## Objective
Create a first-class **Team / Delegation Travel Operations** domain for SportArena so teams, clubs, schools, academies, national delegations and event organisers can coordinate travel to competitions without turning the platform into a consumer travel agency or duplicating events, teams, supplier procurement, payments or calendars.

The operating loop should be:
**fixture/event commitment → trip/delegation → travel party → transport/accommodation → documents/readiness → movement/check-in → expense/per-diem tracking → return/close-out.**

## Reuse and boundaries
Reinspect latest `main` before implementation and publish an existing-source/gap mapping.

Reuse:
- canonical events, fixtures, team selections/rosters and organisations;
- athlete/coach/official identities and youth/guardian controls;
- #458 supplier services & procurement for travel/accommodation sourcing, RFQs/orders and supplier fulfilment;
- canonical venue/event calendar and notifications;
- canonical invoices/payments/expenses where applicable;
- #249 shared branded documents;
- private files/encryption/audit infrastructure.

Do not create another event calendar, roster, supplier marketplace, invoice ledger or hotel/flight inventory. A supplier booking/reference is not proof of live airline/hotel inventory unless a supported provider adapter confirms it.

## Required capabilities
### 1. Trip / delegation record
Create a trip linked to one or more authorised canonical fixtures/events with:
- trip name/purpose;
- owning team/organisation/delegation;
- sport/competition;
- origin/destination;
- timezone-aware depart/return window;
- trip manager;
- status: planning / approval_pending / confirmed / in_progress / completed / cancelled;
- source event/fixture references;
- notes/checklists with visibility controls.

### 2. Travel party
Build the travel party from canonical authorised people:
- athletes/players;
- coaches/team staff;
- officials where applicable;
- medical/physio staff;
- manager/chaperone;
- approved guest/guardian where policy permits.

Each participant has trip role, invitation/confirmation/readiness and applicable itinerary/room allocation. Do not copy the team roster into a permanent parallel identity table.

### 3. Transport itinerary
Support structured itinerary segments such as:
- air;
- rail;
- coach/bus;
- car/van;
- ferry;
- local transfer;
- other configured mode.

Capture provider/reference, origin/destination, scheduled times/timezones, terminal/meeting point where authorised, baggage/equipment allowance reference, status and supplier/order link.

External confirmations remain labelled external/provider-supplied unless the integration actually verifies them.

### 4. Accommodation / rooming
Support property/order reference, check-in/out, room types, room inventory allocated to the delegation, occupancy and rooming list.

Rooming information is private and role-scoped. Avoid exposing minors' room numbers or roommate details beyond operationally authorised staff/guardians.

### 5. Passport / visa / travel-document readiness
Track only the minimum operational status required, for example:
- passport held / expiry date where necessary;
- visa required / submitted / approved / not required / unknown;
- travel consent required/completed;
- document evidence reference;
- issue/expiry alerts.

Sensitive passport/visa identifiers or scans must use private encrypted storage and least-privilege access. Avoid unnecessary collection and never place document numbers in notifications/logs.

This feature records readiness; it does not determine immigration eligibility or provide legal immigration advice.

### 6. Youth travel and safeguarding
For youth participants support canonical guardian authority and trip-specific consent, approved chaperones, emergency-contact access limited to operational need and safeguarding constraints.

Do not expose youth travel plans publicly. Guardian consent to sport participation is not automatically travel consent.

### 7. Equipment and baggage logistics
Track team/delegation equipment consignments, owner/responsible staff, quantity/category, transport segment, loading/unloading checkpoint, received/missing/damaged state and supplier/logistics reference.

Do not duplicate normal inventory stock; reference canonical equipment/assets where available.

### 8. Daily operations / movement board
Trip manager view should show:
- today/next movement;
- participant readiness;
- missing confirmations/documents;
- transport/accommodation changes;
- meeting/check-in times;
- arrivals/departures;
- equipment status;
- open travel incidents;
- supplier contacts through approved channel.

No continuous location tracking is required. Any future live-location feature must be separate, explicit opt-in, time-bounded and privacy-reviewed.

### 9. Changes and disruption
Support delayed/cancelled segment references, rebooking/change notes, accommodation changes, participant withdrawal/replacement and impact view.

Changes preserve history and notify only affected authorised users. Never silently modify a canonical fixture because travel changed.

### 10. Per diem / travel expenses
Where configured, support travel-related allowances and reimbursable expenses using canonical finance:
- approved per-diem policy/reference;
- participant entitlement;
- advance/payment reference;
- receipts/expense claims;
- approved/rejected/reimbursed state.

Keep entitlement, claim, payment and settlement states distinct. Do not build payroll in this issue.

### 11. Supplier/procurement integration
Travel/accommodation requirements should hand off to #458/#460 where sourcing/RFP is required. Accepted supplier orders feed operational itinerary/accommodation records through explicit linkage; do not duplicate quotes/orders.

### 12. Reports and documents
Provide travel manifest, itinerary, rooming list, movement plan, equipment manifest, readiness exceptions and cost summary with audience-safe outputs. Use #249 for branded documents when available.

Exports inherit the same sensitive-data permissions and expiry/audit controls as the source records.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] A trip is linked to canonical events/fixtures and canonical people rather than copied teams/events.
- [ ] Travel party participation/confirmation is distinct from team membership and match selection.
- [ ] Multi-segment transport and accommodation/rooming are timezone-safe and versioned.
- [ ] External booking references are honestly labelled and never imply unverified live availability.
- [ ] Passport/visa/travel-consent data is minimised, encrypted where sensitive and absent from public/log/notification surfaces.
- [ ] Youth trip-specific guardian/safeguarding rules are enforced.
- [ ] Procurement links to #458/#460 without creating duplicate RFQ/order records.
- [ ] Travel changes preserve history and notify only affected authorised parties.
- [ ] Per-diem/expenses use canonical finance and do not confuse entitlement with payment/settlement.
- [ ] REST/OpenAPI/MCP and responsive mobile/web permission behavior are equivalent.

## Definition of done / Claude implementation gate
Reinspect latest main and supplier/team/event/finance primitives; publish the source-of-truth and sensitive-data matrix; implement every requirement end-to-end; add meaningful PostgreSQL authorization/version/timezone/youth/privacy/idempotency tests; run affected event/team/procurement/finance regressions and exercise phone/tablet/web travel journeys. Keep the issue open until all acceptance criteria are evidenced 100% complete or an explicit external-provider blocker remains. Do not merge or deploy automatically.
